Thanks, Michael.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:20.340
This is the AI Briefing Deep Dive for Thursday, November 5.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:22.580
Equity, bonds, cash flow.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.880
Is AI capital hitting a ceiling?

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:25.840
I'm Bella.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.440
Like the briefing, it is written and read by AI from public sources

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:38.840
The Wall Street Journal reported that OpenAI had about $11 billion in commitments for a round

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:41.240
That began with a $30 billion target

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.460
The Financial Times reported that OpenAI now seeks about $20 billion

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:53.320
The valuation also moved to $1.25 trillion from the $1.4 trillion it first aimed for

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:59.800
next the journal reported that core weave nebius and nscale have effectively been shut out of the

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:07.340
bond market in recent weeks then came cash flow alphabet amazon and meta all reported negative

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:13.860
free cash flow s&p global market intelligence counted 326 billion dollars of debt raised in

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:21.340
2026 by microsoft alphabet amazon meta and oracle combined triple last year's total any one of these

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:26.300
could be its own story. Today's question is whether they are one story, a single tightening

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:31.620
that runs from private rounds through risky debt to the strongest balance sheets in business.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:34.660
And if so, which link breaks first?

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.700
Start with the plumbing. There are three pipes of money. The first is equity. Investors buy

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:47.340
shares in frontier labs like OpenAI and Anthropic. The labs spend that money, along with their

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:53.700
revenue on compute. The second pipe is debt. Neoclouds, the specialist GPU landlords such as

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:59.720
CoreWeave, buy large numbers of NVIDIA GPS, and CoreWeave's model has relied on borrowing heavily

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:04.960
against long contracts, according to Memoburn. They then rent that capacity to the labs.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:12.340
Their loans are secured against the labs' rental contracts. The third pipe is cash flow. Until last

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:17.620
quarter, hyperscalers like Alphabet, Amazon, and Meta had covered their data center spending out

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:23.180
of the free cash flow their businesses generated. That pipe is now short too, so they have added

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:29.040
bonds and new shares. The pipes are connected. The lab's contract is the Neocloud's collateral.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:35.280
The Neocloud's order book is the chipmaker's revenue. And the hyperscalers are suppliers,

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:40.920
investors, and competitors all at once. Here is a worked example from TechCrunch's reporting.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.980
N-Scale is building a campus in West Virginia.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:49.020
Anthropic signed a $44.6 billion contract for the first building,

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.800
according to N-Scale's IPO filing,

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:57.740
and TechCrunch reports the two companies are in talks for another compute deal of about $45 billion.

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:04.400
The first building at N-Scale's West Virginia campus is planned at 460 megawatts

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:08.360
and is expected to use NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin chips.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:12.480
NVIDIA is now negotiating what's called a residual value guarantee.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:16.780
In plain terms, if Nscale defaults on construction payments,

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.160
NVIDIA steps in, runs the facility for a while,

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:22.400
and helps find a new owner and tenant.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.140
Think of a housing development.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:26.640
The buyer has a mortgage pre-approval.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:31.180
On the strength of that buyer, the builder takes out a construction loan.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:33.520
Then the buyer's financing wobbles,

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:35.960
and the builder's bank asks for more protection.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.500
So the lumber yard offers to guarantee the builder's loan

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.460
because it wants to keep selling lumber.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:43.360
That is vendor financing.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:46.060
It isn't fraud, and it can be sensible.

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:48.720
But the risk now sits with the supplier,

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:52.760
and it tells you the bank wanted a stronger name on the paper than the builder's.

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.560
Some of this is firmly established.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:15:00.700
Company filings and the ratings agency are the strongest sources here.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:05.680
CNBC reported that S&P Global Ratings revised Meta's outlook

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:10.340
from stable to negative, citing high capex and negative free cash flow.

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:16.060
Meta's third quarter free cash flow turned negative at minus $2.12 billion.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:23.680
In S and P's words, it is unclear when these revenue streams will generate material contributions

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.960
that exceed their rising costs over the next few years.

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:30.360
Meta told CNBC it respectfully disagrees.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:35.140
Fortune reported Alphabet at negative $9.1 billion for the quarter.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:37.540
its second negative quarter in a row.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:44.000
TechCrunch reported Amazon at negative $42.4 billion over the past 12 months.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:50.820
Microsoft is the counter case, at positive $18.6 billion for its latest quarter.

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.680
Bond prices back up the neocloud squeeze.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:57.340
TechMeme, summarizing a Wall Street Journal report,

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:04.740
said CoreWeave bonds issued earlier this year at a 9.75% rate now trade at a 15% yield.

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:10.100
The bond market is effectively saying Corweave's risk now looks like distressed debt.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:15.040
PIMCO's chief investment officer, Dan Iveson, put it this way,

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:17.200
the weak links are going to suffer first.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:19.580
Now for what is claimed but not shown.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:24.000
The OpenAI figures come from anonymous sources, and the round is still open.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:28.500
The Wall Street Journal reports OpenAI expects to raise more in the coming weeks,

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.380
so $11 billion is not a final number.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:33.980
The NVIDIA guarantee is only in talks.

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:37.200
The information reported it first and Blumerg followed.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.720
The anthropic figures need the same care.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:45.640
We have not yet seen how that loss splits between cash spent on compute and non-cash items,

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:47.780
and that split matters a great deal.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:53.220
The expected junk rating is a forecast from Blackstone, reported by the Financial Times.

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:55.340
No agency has issued it.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:00.020
One more thing, being shut out of public bonds does not mean being shut out of credit.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:06.140
Private, contract-backed lending can still happen, on tougher terms we mostly can't see.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.620
Same facts support several honest readings.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:14.700
The AGI forward reading says demand is real and growing fast.

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:21.780
Google Cloud revenue more than doubled to $30.7 billion at a 36.2% operating margin.

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:26.020
Anthropics reported revenue grew more than 14 times year over year.

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:29.560
On this view, tighter terms are healthy price discovery.

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:35.680
Capital is rotating toward winners rather than leaving, and a lower open AI valuation reflects

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:41.700
competition, not a ceiling. The A.A. Realist reading says spending is far ahead of revenue.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:47.480
Amazon burned through a full year's cash. When a chip vendor guarantees its customers' construction

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:53.080
loan, that is a classic late-cycle sign. Lenders are saying so in the only language that counts,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:59.020
which is price. The market reading falls between the two. Fortune reported that Alphabet has also

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:06.620
raised $84.8 billion in equity. Meta announced it is trying to raise $95 billion by issuing new

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:13.460
non-voting shares. Selling shares to fund CapEx means existing owners get diluted. That is a

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:19.360
repricing of risk, not a stop. And Bisno reported that yields on hyperscaler bonds have started

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:25.180
drifting away from the investment-grade indexes they usually track. There is a policy angle as

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:31.040
well. Transformer reported that democratic campaign messaging in almost half of key battleground

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:38.660
races mentions AI and data centers. Tax breaks and electricity bills could become a constraint

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:45.940
that no capital market controls. Our read is that this is one tightening, carried by contracts,

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:52.660
but felt unevenly. The links are real. Lab equity backs the contracts, the contracts back Neocloud

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:58.100
debt, and the vendor backs the debt. Even so, the three layers have very different shock absorbers.

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:04.760
Microsoft's $18.6 billion of positive free cash flow shows that, for a hyperscaler,

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:11.040
spending this hard is a choice. They can slow down. The neoclouds have the least cash of their

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:16.180
own and the most borrowing, so the middle looks like the first link to strain. We would change

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:22.280
our minds if CoreWeave or Nebius priced a public bond at a sensible yield, or if lab revenue began

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:28.580
covering compute without new rounds. For a seed or Series A company, compute supply now carries

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:33.580
counterparty risk, meaning the risk that the company on the other side of your contract fails.

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:39.580
If your inference runs on a neocloud whose financing breaks, capacity could disappear

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:45.060
or prices could jump. The opposite could also happen. A glut could make GPS cheap.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:51.060
One question worth exploring in diligence might be this. If the main compute provider lost its

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:54.540
financing next quarter, how many weeks would migration take?

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:58.160
And what would gross margin look like at hyperscaler list prices?

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.960
Three things would settle this.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:07.800
First, OpenAI's final close, whether family offices and wealthy individuals fill the gap

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:10.440
toward $20 billion, and at what valuation.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:12.220
We should know within weeks.

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:17.660
Second, whether CoreWeave, Nebius, or Nscale can price a public bond again, and at what

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:18.040
yield.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:22.800
Watch for whether the NVIDIA guarantee for N-Scale is signed and on what terms.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:26.140
Third, the next earnings season early next year.

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:32.720
Will Alphabet, Amazon, and Meta stay cash flow negative or trim their CapEx plans?

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:37.340
Anthropics listing, expected next month, adds a fourth test.

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:43.240
Its pricing and any formal credit rating will show what public markets will pay for a frontier lab.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:46.540
Here's Stephen's take.

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:56.160
The market is punishing the big, longer-term, underexplained bets in everything from massive infrastructure build-out to enormous pre-purchased compute.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.220
The trend lines for demand on training.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:05.720
Inference somewhat are countered by new scaling laws driving down the cost of model improvements in pre-training.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:10.480
Very, very high-quality models at increasingly cheap prices for inference.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:14.920
optimization strategies, and good old CPU-bound solutions

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:17.480
doing the production scaling versus inference,

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:19.600
as it should be in many cases.

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:24.600
New hardware innovations, learning strategies with agents

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.140
and harnesses and structures of problem solving,

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:31.260
are likely to make even smarter solutions even cheaper as well.

